#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230032} :activity/id #uuid "ad098e4e-ddaa-5a8c-8676-9f6c2a1554d5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049727 :topic/id #uuid "0e6d0d09-5c9c-596c-83ce-4f15c32fd909" :topic/math-academy-id 6689} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230186 :tutorial/content "We know how to apply the trapezoidal method to initial value problems that are linear in $y.$ Can we apply it to more complex initial value problems, such as\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2 - y^4, \\qquad y(0)=1?\n$$\n\nYes, we can. To do so, we define a function $g$ such that the next $y$-value, $y_\\text{new},$ satisfies the equation $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$ Then, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's apply the trapezoidal method to the IVP above with step size $\\Delta x = 0.5.$\n\nWe begin as usual. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the update formula for $\\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, there is no simple algebraic rearrangement to isolate $y_\\text{new},$ so solving exactly is impractical.\nInstead, we define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that $x,$ $y,$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants at each step, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function: $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ Therefore, to find $y_\\text{new},$ we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 0.5 = 0.5.$ So, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25\\cdot (1)^{4}-1-0.5\\cdot (0)^{2}-0.5\\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9cd9bd37-323b-5d8a-8d1f-4d8924a59da0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14495 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3c9d3bf2-ca7c-52cb-b895-adf1cf0cbed3" :step/math-academy-id 594484 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228909 :question/id #uuid "9148676f-5899-539c-9b95-4a0d8d225056" :question/math-academy-id "e-21679"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050483}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391387}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "33217370-2caf-5db2-a016-cc53daad6665" :step/math-academy-id 594485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228908 :question/id #uuid "d77c4a1b-863b-57ad-be77-44f2d802f554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377189}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "95fba8e6-749f-55be-9019-f637bd866173" :step/math-academy-id 594488}] :activity/title "Using the Trapezoidal Method With Newton's Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229255}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413441} :activity/id #uuid "8aa994fd-2bb6-5474-8e58-23decc01455c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049727 :topic/id #uuid "0e6d0d09-5c9c-596c-83ce-4f15c32fd909" :topic/math-academy-id 6689} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a65f23d9-ece1-5d94-9f1b-4fa3ff21de35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391389} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d2a40-8db7-58a2-965b-5f8b9284015a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391390} {:answer/id #uuid "04c655f7-87ce-528a-8800-3f6394ecdb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391391} {:answer/id #uuid "f96ae150-da92-5738-ba1d-d4d1763de606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391392} {:answer/id #uuid "838f71c2-360e-538a-95d7-8b3b5dc7a597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391393}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391392} :answer-field/id #uuid "df1f67de-316f-5c29-b742-1caa63eae0d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3e7b77a-8724-5c98-839a-9b13b32a04b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334077" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "17e3c9fb-5004-5b6c-9abb-f74c6a155256" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a87b41-81d8-5f96-86cd-025b9c0b9dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305164} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6a5683-9ee1-5947-b947-fcf1a8f2e101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305768} {:answer/id #uuid "70e24b12-cff1-552a-8352-837e273c1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306228} {:answer/id #uuid "779542c4-9213-5e22-a1d3-6b9f712a5f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306287} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1085e2-2118-5ddc-a2d2-87792732c5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305164} :answer-field/id #uuid "5810ab1d-8820-5650-86eb-5e9d43366c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0755b082-c081-5233-b79a-1dd852af7280" :question/math-academy-id "q-334080" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example  $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({x}^{2}-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-1+{2}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0+4-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c10d508e-2b41-5faa-9b45-2844e60e0e0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413443}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fc8c65-780c-5c34-b5be-571c2a7b85bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305470} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f75cf12-8b61-51e6-bed9-8d88af4bba7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cdf480b-e736-5530-b72b-9719ec66981a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334469" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x-{y}^{3})+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-1.5\\cdot 1-1.5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5-1-1.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179\\,747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179\\,509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "fd33c32d-69d3-5f03-b417-6f28a9fbbbc5"}] :activity/title "Using the Trapezoidal Method With Newton's Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413440}]}
